      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© The Author(s) 2015.",
        "AbstractText": "This investigation explored the association between anxiety sensitivity and attentional bias for threatening information in children and adolescents (N\u2009=\u200940). Participants completed a pictorial version of the visual-probe task, featuring pain-related, health-threat and general-threat images presented for 500 and 1250\u2009ms. Regression analyses revealed significant associations between anxiety sensitivity and attentional bias towards pain-related images presented for 500\u2009ms and between state anxiety and attentional bias towards general-threat images presented for 1250\u2009ms. These results suggest that in children and adolescents, anxiety sensitivity is associated with attentional bias for negative information of personal relevance."
      },
